Report out of memory instead of returning NULL from a throwing operator new.

mimalloc implements the C++ `operator new` family (alloc-override.c) and is
Bun's global allocator on Linux. Bun compiles without exceptions and installs
no `std::new_handler`, so the no-exceptions arm of mi_try_new_handler reaches
`h == NULL`, cannot throw, and returns false. mi_theap_try_new then returns
NULL to a caller the standard promised memory, and the caller writes through
it: a refused `std::string` growth faults at address 0x0.

With -DMI_NEW_OOM=<function> that arm calls the named noreturn function
instead. `new (std::nothrow)` still returns NULL.

--- a/src/alloc.c
+++ b/src/alloc.c
@@ -759,6 +759,14 @@
 -------------------------------------------------------*/
 #define MI_TRY_NEW_MAX (4)
 
+#if defined(MI_NEW_OOM) && !defined(_CPPUNWIND) && !defined(__cpp_exceptions)
+// Reports out of memory and does not return. Stands in for the
+// `throw std::bad_alloc()` that a build without exceptions cannot do, so a
+// throwing `new` never returns NULL to a caller the standard promised memory.
+mi_decl_externc mi_decl_noreturn void MI_NEW_OOM(void);
+#define MI_HAS_NEW_OOM 1
+#endif
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 #include <new>
 static bool mi_try_new_handler(bool nothrow) {
@@ -774,6 +782,10 @@
     if (!nothrow) {
       throw std::bad_alloc();
     }
+    #elif defined(MI_HAS_NEW_OOM)
+    if (!nothrow) {
+      MI_NEW_OOM();
+    }
     #else
     MI_UNUSED(nothrow);
     #endif
